    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d273">

                <pb facs="0238" n="188" sameAs="frus:frus1918Russiav03/pg188"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">File No. 861.51/140</note>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">The Ambassador in Russia</hi> (<hi rendition="#italic"
                        >Francis</hi>) <hi rendition="#italic">to the Secretary of State</hi></head>
                <opener>
                    <seg rendition="#center">[Telegram—Extract]<anchor n="2" xml:id="d273-f1"
                        /></seg>
                </opener>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Petrograd</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1917-04-29T20:00:00+02:31"><hi rendition="#italic">April 29, 1917,
                                8 p.m.</hi></date>
                        <lb/> [<hi rendition="#italic">Received April 30, 6.30 a.m.</hi>]</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>1236. … Callers [two Russian Ministers] told me Ministry resented suggestion of
                    British Ambassador that Siberian Railway be turned over to us, saying Russia
                    does not need nurses but had agreed directly with us concerning Vladivostok and
                    had <pb facs="0239" n="189" xml:id="pg189"/> candidly told me yesterday of their
                    wishes and requirements in White Sea. You see their sensitiveness especially [as
                    concerns] England.</p>
                <closer>
                    <signed><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                            >Francis</hi></signed>
                </closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
